WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.932 --> 00:00:03.849
(mysterious music)

00:00:04.990 --> 00:00:06.760
- [Robotic voice] Hello, everyone.

00:00:06.760 --> 00:00:09.820
- [Announcer] In January 2012,
a mysterious organization

00:00:09.820 --> 00:00:11.770
released an intricate
puzzle on the internet

00:00:11.770 --> 00:00:14.723
saying it was looking for
highly intelligent individuals.

00:00:15.740 --> 00:00:18.850
The group issued a new puzzle
the following two years

00:00:18.850 --> 00:00:21.500
until its latest one couldn't be solved.

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:22.940
It remains a mystery to those

00:00:22.940 --> 00:00:25.543
who still are trying to
crack it-- four years later.

00:00:28.230 --> 00:00:30.060
No one knows who's behind the puzzle

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:32.093
or how the latest piece can be decoded.

00:00:33.850 --> 00:00:36.810
Why do these solvers
continue against all odds?

00:00:36.810 --> 00:00:38.770
And who might give us insight

00:00:38.770 --> 00:00:41.843
into the mind behind Cicada 3301.

00:00:42.870 --> 00:00:44.110
- [Robotic voice] The key has always been

00:00:44.110 --> 00:00:46.380
right in front of your eyes.

00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:47.843
Good luck, 3301.

00:00:57.262 --> 00:01:00.179
(futuristic music)

00:01:02.658 --> 00:01:06.158
(train running on tracks)

00:01:08.829 --> 00:01:09.720
- As a kid, I believed

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:12.120
I could sell a little seed of curiosity

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:13.370
into the minds of people.

00:01:16.850 --> 00:01:19.560
To think about how to
create a society in which

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:22.143
the open exchange of
information is encouraged.

00:01:24.815 --> 00:01:28.565
(train station announcement)

00:01:29.810 --> 00:01:31.383
As a kid, I show anything that is there.

00:01:31.383 --> 00:01:32.920
I really like to get people

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:34.970
to think about how things fit together

00:01:35.860 --> 00:01:38.200
and how they can be rearranged

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:39.713
in order to create new things.

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:45.770
It may put you on the map
to intelligence agencies

00:01:45.770 --> 00:01:47.840
because you dabble with
breaking encryption.

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:48.810
You dabble with a certain

00:01:48.810 --> 00:01:50.873
kind of agriculture and e-flux.

00:01:51.970 --> 00:01:56.283
You expose yourself and you
may be a threat to some people.

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:01.883
I'm Brother Box;

00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:05.040
actually I don't wanna talk about my age.

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:07.397
People are not supposed
to know my age, actually.

00:02:07.397 --> 00:02:11.100
But I will just keep a brief
introduction about myself.

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:13.450
My name is Brother Box, I live in Germany.

00:02:13.450 --> 00:02:14.833
I study IT Security.

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:19.320
I remember I played my first
computer game at around four,

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:20.870
and I didn't understand much

00:02:20.870 --> 00:02:22.080
because they were all in English

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:24.030
and I kinda had to guess my way around.

00:02:25.180 --> 00:02:27.534
And that was actually a
motivation for me to learn English

00:02:27.534 --> 00:02:30.700
because I was tired of asking
grown-ups what things meant.

00:02:30.700 --> 00:02:33.500
Only my brother knows that
I am, at all, into Cicada.

00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:35.340
I keep it away from most people

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:37.773
because most people don't connect with us.

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:43.590
Hackers are driven by
the urge to know things

00:02:43.590 --> 00:02:46.040
and mostly about the things
that they don't know.

00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:49.420
'Cause a hacker considers
the fact that someone

00:02:49.420 --> 00:02:52.270
may forbid you to know
something as a threat

00:02:52.270 --> 00:02:53.643
to their own curiosity.

00:02:55.290 --> 00:02:57.960
I believe that the people
that are behind Cicada

00:02:57.960 --> 00:03:00.870
are driven by much the same mindset.

00:03:00.870 --> 00:03:02.460
When I started with Cicada,

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:04.970
I was the guy who asked the questions.

00:03:04.970 --> 00:03:06.830
I was the guy who was slow at learning.

00:03:06.830 --> 00:03:09.793
Now, I am the person that
gets asked these questions.

00:03:10.790 --> 00:03:13.050
I like to believe that I
am one of the most rigorous

00:03:13.050 --> 00:03:14.950
and thorough members of our community.

00:03:16.030 --> 00:03:18.313
People value my word and my judgment.

00:03:20.090 --> 00:03:22.610
Our community is a bit different.

00:03:22.610 --> 00:03:25.510
When you're part of us,
you're kinda part of us

00:03:25.510 --> 00:03:27.913
and over time you form this bond.

00:03:27.913 --> 00:03:28.746
(mysterious music)

00:03:28.746 --> 00:03:31.163
(chattering)

00:03:39.700 --> 00:03:44.450
I first got to know Onon in
2012, but I've never met him.

00:03:44.450 --> 00:03:47.040
He is not necessarily a
part of the core team,

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:50.143
but I think this entire operation
would be poor without him.

00:03:51.429 --> 00:03:53.008
(mysterious music)

00:03:53.008 --> 00:03:55.425
(chattering)

00:03:56.559 --> 00:04:00.642
(speaking in a foreign language)

00:04:09.957 --> 00:04:13.470
- Cicada puzzlers usually
wrap up within a month.

00:04:13.470 --> 00:04:15.400
This puzzle has persisted

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:18.920
and denied us any resolve for three years.

00:04:18.920 --> 00:04:21.690
I believe Liber Primus is solvable,

00:04:21.690 --> 00:04:24.990
but at this point only a
very fresh mind can do it.

00:04:24.990 --> 00:04:28.190
Someone who looks at things
in a completely different way.

00:04:28.190 --> 00:04:29.023
(mysterious music)

00:04:29.023 --> 00:04:33.106
(speaking in a foreign language)

00:05:17.937 --> 00:05:20.770
(uplifting music)

00:05:23.277 --> 00:05:27.360
(speaking in a foreign language)

00:05:29.920 --> 00:05:32.190
- To effectively solve Cicada,

00:05:32.190 --> 00:05:34.440
you have to have a very inquisitive mind

00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:36.463
and you have to be technically capable.

00:05:37.530 --> 00:05:39.650
And I believe that in Cicada's mindset,

00:05:39.650 --> 00:05:41.850
it was important to connect these people

00:05:41.850 --> 00:05:43.103
into one central hub.

00:05:45.020 --> 00:05:47.750
We took a lot of time and a lot of effort

00:05:47.750 --> 00:05:50.760
trying to solve Liber Primus,

00:05:50.760 --> 00:05:54.340
and I would like to resolve
that piece of their legacy,

00:05:54.340 --> 00:05:56.183
so maybe we can all move on someday.

00:05:58.620 --> 00:06:01.005
It is that itch that you can't scratch,

00:06:01.005 --> 00:06:02.705
and you want to scratch it so bad.

00:06:07.210 --> 00:06:09.330
- Hi, okay, so take a look.

00:06:09.330 --> 00:06:12.000
So this here is this message.

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:13.900
Well, in 2016, it was a year and a half

00:06:13.900 --> 00:06:15.600
before they released this picture.

00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:18.980
Do you see this large box?

00:06:18.980 --> 00:06:20.400
- Yeah, that's outcast data, isn't it?

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:23.890
- Yeah, so doesn't this
look a lot like that tree,

00:06:23.890 --> 00:06:27.190
when I zoomed in on it on page 32?

00:06:27.190 --> 00:06:28.820
- Maybe that's an angle that we should

00:06:28.820 --> 00:06:30.030
at least think about.

00:06:30.030 --> 00:06:31.860
You see how on page, what is it?

00:06:31.860 --> 00:06:35.800
57, we are using just
good old gematria, right,

00:06:35.800 --> 00:06:37.860
which I did not believe
to be a coincidence.

00:06:37.860 --> 00:06:41.740
In 2013, we had gotten the translation

00:06:41.740 --> 00:06:43.140
from runes to English

00:06:43.140 --> 00:06:45.040
and we didn't know what
to do with them back then

00:06:45.040 --> 00:06:46.490
because we had new runes.

00:06:46.490 --> 00:06:49.350
In 2014, we finally got the first runes.

00:06:49.350 --> 00:06:51.240
On the second to last page of the runes,

00:06:51.240 --> 00:06:54.527
they said, "There is
a page of the deep web

00:06:54.527 --> 00:06:58.320
"and it is the duty of every
pilgrim to find this page."

00:06:58.320 --> 00:06:59.550
- For the longest time,

00:06:59.550 --> 00:07:02.920
we had no idea what it could be.

00:07:02.920 --> 00:07:05.580
We tried a lot of stuff on Tor;

00:07:05.580 --> 00:07:07.993
scanning Tor to find websites.

00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:11.010
We found some interesting stuff,

00:07:11.010 --> 00:07:12.810
but not Cicada (chuckles).

00:07:12.810 --> 00:07:15.510
- The deep web is a vast and inept space.

00:07:15.510 --> 00:07:17.950
And unless you know what
you want to find in it,

00:07:17.950 --> 00:07:19.860
you're probably not going to find it.

00:07:19.860 --> 00:07:23.810
- What if we use plain
text on page 57 as the key?

00:07:23.810 --> 00:07:28.130
On those pages 23 through 26,

00:07:28.130 --> 00:07:29.730
'cause it's a mirrored mayfly.

00:07:29.730 --> 00:07:32.490
The same mayfly that's on 57.

00:07:32.490 --> 00:07:33.460
- Cool idea.

00:07:33.460 --> 00:07:35.100
- Literally, anything usable

00:07:35.100 --> 00:07:37.170
out of those possibly repeated words,

00:07:37.170 --> 00:07:40.287
it is gonna be the most
anyone's got on this in years.

00:07:42.859 --> 00:07:45.776
(mysterious music)

00:07:50.290 --> 00:07:52.230
- Are the keys to
breaking the Liber Primus

00:07:52.230 --> 00:07:53.283
hidden in history?

00:07:57.230 --> 00:07:59.720
The importance of
cryptography and code breaking

00:07:59.720 --> 00:08:00.843
goes back millennia.

00:08:02.830 --> 00:08:05.090
As long as we've had
a need to communicate,

00:08:05.090 --> 00:08:08.226
we've also had a need to keep
those communications secret.

00:08:08.226 --> 00:08:11.143
(mysterious music)

00:08:12.610 --> 00:08:15.020
Even Julius Caesar would
send in coded messages

00:08:15.020 --> 00:08:17.220
for his generals to
read on the battlefield.

00:08:19.910 --> 00:08:21.560
In much more recent history,

00:08:21.560 --> 00:08:22.820
the world as we know it

00:08:22.820 --> 00:08:24.420
might have been dramatically different,

00:08:24.420 --> 00:08:25.410
if it weren't for England's

00:08:25.410 --> 00:08:27.300
own group of cryptographic solvers,

00:08:27.300 --> 00:08:30.050
disrupting German's communications
during World War II.

00:08:32.260 --> 00:08:34.160
Bletchley Park was home
to their top-secret

00:08:34.160 --> 00:08:36.600
code breaking operation during the war.

00:08:36.600 --> 00:08:37.560
The German high command

00:08:37.560 --> 00:08:39.010
would send encrypted messages

00:08:39.010 --> 00:08:41.786
all over the battlefields
of Europe and North Africa.

00:08:41.786 --> 00:08:44.536
(dramatic music)

00:08:46.764 --> 00:08:50.310
- By 1945, you've got nearly
10,000 people working here.

00:08:50.310 --> 00:08:51.950
Three shifts, 24 hours a day,

00:08:51.950 --> 00:08:54.770
processing huge amounts of intelligence.

00:08:54.770 --> 00:08:56.340
- They were confident in the security

00:08:56.340 --> 00:08:58.650
of these highly sensitive communications

00:08:58.650 --> 00:09:00.500
because of their secret weapon.

00:09:00.500 --> 00:09:02.453
It was called Enigma.

00:09:03.850 --> 00:09:05.750
- Oh my gosh, that's it?

00:09:05.750 --> 00:09:06.583
- That's it.

00:09:06.583 --> 00:09:07.660
That's an enigma machine.

00:09:07.660 --> 00:09:08.710
It's not a radio set,

00:09:08.710 --> 00:09:10.230
it doesn't communicate,

00:09:10.230 --> 00:09:11.333
it just encrypts.

00:09:12.700 --> 00:09:13.880
I'ma show you how that happens.

00:09:13.880 --> 00:09:14.713
- [Man With Glasses] Yes.

00:09:14.713 --> 00:09:15.546
- [David] What this does is

00:09:15.546 --> 00:09:17.070
you press the letter, any key you like.

00:09:17.070 --> 00:09:17.970
I'm gonna press P.

00:09:19.020 --> 00:09:21.070
You see Y is lit up.
- Right.

00:09:21.070 --> 00:09:22.480
- The clever part is

00:09:22.480 --> 00:09:23.710
if I press P again

00:09:25.120 --> 00:09:25.953
I don't get Y,

00:09:25.953 --> 00:09:26.820
I get a different letter.

00:09:26.820 --> 00:09:28.040
For every key pressed

00:09:28.040 --> 00:09:29.720
it completely changes the encryption.

00:09:29.720 --> 00:09:32.490
Knowing how it functioned
was the start of the story,

00:09:32.490 --> 00:09:34.740
but it's not the end of
the story by any means.

00:09:34.740 --> 00:09:36.740
What helped them break this machine

00:09:36.740 --> 00:09:38.670
to understand the messages sent on it

00:09:38.670 --> 00:09:40.750
was to understand, basically,

00:09:40.750 --> 00:09:42.769
how the operators misused it.

00:09:42.769 --> 00:09:44.020
(mysterious music)

00:09:44.020 --> 00:09:46.800
The key to breaking Enigma was cribbing.

00:09:46.800 --> 00:09:49.400
If you could guess part of a message,

00:09:49.400 --> 00:09:52.150
and you knew what letters
spelled that plain text,

00:09:52.150 --> 00:09:54.670
you could then offer that
up against the cipher text

00:09:54.670 --> 00:09:57.040
and start to establish relationships.

00:09:57.040 --> 00:09:58.770
One of the mistakes the Germans made

00:09:58.770 --> 00:10:01.750
was to be predictable in their language.

00:10:01.750 --> 00:10:04.420
Common words were things
like weather forecast.

00:10:04.420 --> 00:10:06.080
The Germans had weather
ships out the Atlantic

00:10:06.080 --> 00:10:08.260
that sent weather
forecasts back every day.

00:10:08.260 --> 00:10:09.710
So, everyday they're sending a message,

00:10:09.710 --> 00:10:12.070
the first word which is weather forecast.

00:10:12.070 --> 00:10:13.740
If you know what those messages are

00:10:13.740 --> 00:10:15.230
you got a crib into them.

00:10:15.230 --> 00:10:17.630
So, you used the really
dull, boring messages

00:10:17.630 --> 00:10:19.770
that you can guess the content of

00:10:19.770 --> 00:10:22.070
to unlock the ones that
have the real secrecy.

00:10:23.161 --> 00:10:24.700
- [Man With Glasses] How
does the lessons of Enigma

00:10:24.700 --> 00:10:27.020
help us solve Cicada?

00:10:27.020 --> 00:10:29.080
- Just look at the six sequence of runes.

00:10:29.080 --> 00:10:31.200
One option is to use cribs

00:10:31.200 --> 00:10:32.780
to guess phrases that you think

00:10:32.780 --> 00:10:34.493
might be what that message says.

00:10:36.950 --> 00:10:39.070
And if you do that, you
can then see patterns

00:10:39.070 --> 00:10:42.053
between the runes and your
plain text characters.

00:10:44.110 --> 00:10:45.020
And these are the tools

00:10:45.020 --> 00:10:47.470
that cryptographers have
used throughout history.

00:10:50.170 --> 00:10:51.260
- Didn't you say that

00:10:51.260 --> 00:10:53.470
the English cribbed
for German curse words?

00:10:53.470 --> 00:10:54.303
- Yeah, they did.

00:10:54.303 --> 00:10:55.920
They were cribbing for swears in German

00:10:55.920 --> 00:10:57.340
'cause they knew about the length of them

00:10:57.340 --> 00:10:59.820
and the Germans wouldn't
stop swearing at each other.

00:10:59.820 --> 00:11:02.500
That and weather reports
are the two biggest things

00:11:02.500 --> 00:11:03.460
they used for cribbing.

00:11:03.460 --> 00:11:04.830
When trying to figure
out all this (laughs)

00:11:04.830 --> 00:11:08.910
- And also the Heil Hitler...
- (foreign word spoken)

00:11:08.910 --> 00:11:10.100
- So, Box?

00:11:10.100 --> 00:11:11.060
- That's me!

00:11:11.060 --> 00:11:14.490
I'm 12000 percent fed up with outguess.

00:11:14.490 --> 00:11:16.610
I don't ever want to hear
that word ever again.

00:11:16.610 --> 00:11:17.840
- We're just gonna go over

00:11:17.840 --> 00:11:19.680
what we've done in like
the last week or so.

00:11:19.680 --> 00:11:23.330
The long and short of that
is we got this giant list

00:11:23.330 --> 00:11:27.070
which is every giant string of runes

00:11:27.070 --> 00:11:28.110
that you've tried to encrypt.

00:11:28.110 --> 00:11:31.430
Look, a numerical value of
its fitness with English.

00:11:31.430 --> 00:11:34.570
- It looks like it has a great
strength and a great weakness

00:11:34.570 --> 00:11:37.480
because it checks for n-grams in order

00:11:37.480 --> 00:11:40.120
which could be helpful for
partially decrypted words.

00:11:40.120 --> 00:11:42.450
- This was used when they were getting

00:11:42.450 --> 00:11:45.200
partial decryptions on Enigma.

00:11:45.200 --> 00:11:46.270
So that's what we did this weekend.

00:11:46.270 --> 00:11:48.600
I don't remember...

00:11:48.600 --> 00:11:50.780
I don't remember days,
they all blend together.

00:11:50.780 --> 00:11:53.640
- We have done so much automation on this

00:11:53.640 --> 00:11:56.370
that the actual solution, if there is one,

00:11:56.370 --> 00:12:00.620
is probably one that has to
be guided by human brain.

00:12:00.620 --> 00:12:03.040
- We need something more advanced.

00:12:03.040 --> 00:12:04.150
- My partners, we need to put

00:12:04.150 --> 00:12:06.284
our beautiful little heads to it.

00:12:06.284 --> 00:12:08.951
(calming music)

00:12:10.750 --> 00:12:12.030
- Around 11 o'clock at night,

00:12:12.030 --> 00:12:13.410
is usually when we all kinda

00:12:13.410 --> 00:12:14.923
get together for our hangouts.

00:12:16.310 --> 00:12:18.830
We go for maybe three or four hours

00:12:18.830 --> 00:12:19.830
at once every night.

00:12:20.730 --> 00:12:24.763
I also spend some time outside
of it thinking about it.

00:12:26.130 --> 00:12:27.480
There's a lot of that in computing

00:12:27.480 --> 00:12:29.770
where you're not working and typing it

00:12:29.770 --> 00:12:33.590
because you don't really
know yet how to form it.

00:12:33.590 --> 00:12:36.503
You have to think it through
and then you just go.

00:12:38.970 --> 00:12:41.450
People have been so
burned out trying this.

00:12:41.450 --> 00:12:43.600
So many people have thrown up their hands.

00:12:43.600 --> 00:12:44.730
They get frustrated with this

00:12:44.730 --> 00:12:46.923
because it's like a big wall of text.

00:12:49.120 --> 00:12:53.100
If Craig can help us,
even decrypt one page,

00:12:53.100 --> 00:12:57.203
that would be the biggest
break we've had since 2014.

00:12:59.788 --> 00:13:00.621
(door opening)

00:13:00.621 --> 00:13:01.890
- Hi!
- Hey!

00:13:01.890 --> 00:13:02.753
- Come on in.
- How's it going?

00:13:02.753 --> 00:13:05.000
- Onecool is connecting with Craig Bauer,

00:13:05.000 --> 00:13:07.220
one of the world top crypto experts,

00:13:07.220 --> 00:13:09.060
in hopes of gaining some insight

00:13:09.060 --> 00:13:11.023
in order to crack the Liber Primus.

00:13:11.980 --> 00:13:14.957
Bauer's a former resident
historian at the N.S.A.

00:13:14.957 --> 00:13:16.530
And has literally written the book

00:13:16.530 --> 00:13:18.323
on famous unsolved puzzles.

00:13:24.070 --> 00:13:27.470
- Gematria Primus, this
was what they revealed

00:13:27.470 --> 00:13:28.720
through one of the puzzles

00:13:28.720 --> 00:13:31.140
and that was used directly

00:13:31.140 --> 00:13:32.580
to decode some pages.

00:13:32.580 --> 00:13:35.750
So, it's mapping from
rune to English letter.

00:13:35.750 --> 00:13:40.020
Very last page of this, it
translates directly here to P.

00:13:40.020 --> 00:13:41.970
The next one was A.

00:13:41.970 --> 00:13:43.820
So, this says parable.

00:13:43.820 --> 00:13:45.130
- [Craig] So, it was the last page

00:13:45.130 --> 00:13:47.090
the only one that was
a substitution cipher?

00:13:47.090 --> 00:13:48.600
- That's a great question.

00:13:48.600 --> 00:13:49.700
The second to last page

00:13:49.700 --> 00:13:52.800
was encrypted with a stream of numbers.

00:13:52.800 --> 00:13:55.970
So, that gave us the message

00:13:55.970 --> 00:13:59.500
that said we had to seek
out a page on the deep web.

00:13:59.500 --> 00:14:02.640
They said it's everybody's
duty to find this page.

00:14:02.640 --> 00:14:04.530
- I mean, we know that Cicada's obsessed

00:14:04.530 --> 00:14:07.350
with prime numbers forgetting about

00:14:07.350 --> 00:14:09.140
this rune correspondence.

00:14:09.140 --> 00:14:10.010
It sounds like they have

00:14:10.010 --> 00:14:13.220
really modern implementations
like steganography,

00:14:13.220 --> 00:14:15.460
and things you have to
look for through outguess.

00:14:15.460 --> 00:14:16.870
- Yeah.
- It's not just flat out

00:14:16.870 --> 00:14:18.420
math, math, math, right?
- Yeah.

00:14:18.420 --> 00:14:20.720
- And maybe the key is text,

00:14:20.720 --> 00:14:22.570
in which case, Friedman's Attack

00:14:22.570 --> 00:14:25.640
and its generalizations
would be a good approach.

00:14:25.640 --> 00:14:27.550
- [Narrator] William
Friedman was a geneticist

00:14:27.550 --> 00:14:29.740
who took on cryptography as a hobby.

00:14:29.740 --> 00:14:33.190
He later became the first Chief
Cryptologist at the N.S.A.

00:14:33.190 --> 00:14:34.610
Friedman invented a technique

00:14:34.610 --> 00:14:37.570
of putting two texts side by side

00:14:37.570 --> 00:14:39.240
and counting the number of times

00:14:39.240 --> 00:14:40.750
that identical letters appeared

00:14:40.750 --> 00:14:43.660
in the same position in both texts.

00:14:43.660 --> 00:14:45.900
Friedman's Attack looked at the frequency

00:14:45.900 --> 00:14:47.610
certain letters appeared

00:14:47.610 --> 00:14:49.390
and was thereby able to determine

00:14:49.390 --> 00:14:52.890
whether it was a simple or
a complex encoded message.

00:14:52.890 --> 00:14:54.910
All this may not sound like much;

00:14:54.910 --> 00:14:57.670
it greatly reduces the
nearly infinite possibilities

00:14:57.670 --> 00:15:01.160
you have to try on a puzzle
like the Liber Primus.

00:15:01.160 --> 00:15:03.460
- So, Friedman's Attack would basically

00:15:03.460 --> 00:15:05.940
look at the pairings most likely

00:15:05.940 --> 00:15:07.810
to give you the letter you see.

00:15:07.810 --> 00:15:10.210
- So, those very infrequent letters,

00:15:10.210 --> 00:15:11.510
then you can kind of discard

00:15:11.510 --> 00:15:13.670
and focus on the common English letters,

00:15:13.670 --> 00:15:14.690
Right?
- Yes.

00:15:14.690 --> 00:15:16.010
You might be missing a few letters

00:15:16.010 --> 00:15:17.550
where rarer letters came into play,

00:15:17.550 --> 00:15:18.770
but it would just be like reading a book

00:15:18.770 --> 00:15:22.090
with a lot of typos.
- Really, wow.

00:15:22.090 --> 00:15:24.550
That's fascinating 'cause its kind of like

00:15:24.550 --> 00:15:26.160
taking the analysis we've done

00:15:26.160 --> 00:15:28.840
and really taking it a step further.

00:15:28.840 --> 00:15:30.970
'Cause I hadn't, I've never heard of that

00:15:30.970 --> 00:15:32.880
and those are kind of the types of ciphers

00:15:32.880 --> 00:15:34.760
that are great to know about

00:15:34.760 --> 00:15:36.270
because we've really been stuck.

00:15:36.270 --> 00:15:37.620
We can't drop the problem,

00:15:37.620 --> 00:15:39.980
so we're gonna try this

00:15:39.980 --> 00:15:41.750
and I'll kinda get back to the guys

00:15:41.750 --> 00:15:43.550
about your thoughts on it.

00:15:43.550 --> 00:15:44.900
I'm sure you've been in a situation

00:15:44.900 --> 00:15:47.137
where you're like, "I
need to figure it out

00:15:47.137 --> 00:15:49.000
"and I can't just let it go"

00:15:49.000 --> 00:15:51.870
because you're stubborn.
- Sure.

00:15:51.870 --> 00:15:54.110
I'm just pleased that it
gave a little bit of a prod

00:15:54.110 --> 00:15:55.440
to someone to do that

00:15:55.440 --> 00:15:58.020
and now maybe it'll come
full circle and help us.

00:15:58.020 --> 00:15:59.280
If you weren't persistent,

00:15:59.280 --> 00:16:00.750
you wouldn't be attacking ciphers.

00:16:00.750 --> 00:16:01.583
I mean, it demands

00:16:01.583 --> 00:16:03.219
that kind of personality.
- Yeah.

00:16:03.219 --> 00:16:08.219
(calming music)
(crickets chirping)

00:16:12.210 --> 00:16:14.643
- Nox and I are headed
to meet Elonka Dunin.

00:16:16.720 --> 00:16:18.220
She's a noted code breaker

00:16:18.220 --> 00:16:19.610
best known for her expertise

00:16:19.610 --> 00:16:22.223
with the C.I.A.'s Kryptos puzzle.

00:16:24.020 --> 00:16:26.380
She even runs her own solving community,

00:16:26.380 --> 00:16:29.323
attempting to break the
puzzle's famous last section.

00:16:31.855 --> 00:16:33.240
- Can you help us put Cicada

00:16:33.240 --> 00:16:37.430
in some type of context of crypto puzzles,

00:16:37.430 --> 00:16:40.370
of the larger world that
you're so familiar with.

00:16:40.370 --> 00:16:42.190
- You know more about the details

00:16:42.190 --> 00:16:43.100
of what's going on.

00:16:43.100 --> 00:16:46.390
'Cause I have people send me
codes two, three times a week.

00:16:46.390 --> 00:16:47.223
- I can actually show you

00:16:47.223 --> 00:16:48.056
if you want.
- Sure, sure, sure!

00:16:48.056 --> 00:16:50.740
- Typically, the sort of
classical cryptography,

00:16:50.740 --> 00:16:53.160
letter shifting, Vigenère--
all that sort of style

00:16:53.160 --> 00:16:55.020
of cryptography they don't do as much of.

00:16:55.020 --> 00:16:56.580
A lot of what they've done has been like

00:16:56.580 --> 00:16:57.900
RSA break.
- Techy.

00:16:57.900 --> 00:16:59.620
- So this really, we were kind of

00:16:59.620 --> 00:17:01.940
unprepared for what they gave us,

00:17:01.940 --> 00:17:04.810
which is 58 encrypted pages,

00:17:04.810 --> 00:17:06.290
either every section or every page

00:17:06.290 --> 00:17:07.600
has a different encoding,

00:17:07.600 --> 00:17:09.787
like encryption.
- Okay, okay.

00:17:09.787 --> 00:17:12.940
- We've only decrypted two
in (chuckles) three years.

00:17:12.940 --> 00:17:13.990
You're welcome to look through it

00:17:13.990 --> 00:17:15.350
as much as you want.
- Okay.

00:17:15.350 --> 00:17:17.010
- This one, the key was.

00:17:17.010 --> 00:17:19.233
It's running key the length of the page.

00:17:20.438 --> 00:17:23.500
It's the totient of the prime sequence.

00:17:23.500 --> 00:17:25.080
So, it's, yeah.
- Okay

00:17:25.080 --> 00:17:26.457
- Like, you see, yeah.
- Okay.

00:17:26.457 --> 00:17:27.360
- (laughs) You see the issues
- Okay

00:17:27.360 --> 00:17:28.470
- we're having.

00:17:28.470 --> 00:17:31.070
We've been doing a lot with
the law of probabilities of

00:17:31.070 --> 00:17:33.800
quadgrams and trigrams.
- Oooh, okay, good.

00:17:33.800 --> 00:17:35.930
- And--
- You really wanna solve this.

00:17:35.930 --> 00:17:37.920
Yeah, that's great! Okay!

00:17:37.920 --> 00:17:40.330
So, I'm just trying to...

00:17:41.460 --> 00:17:43.220
- It's a lot, I know.
- Build my mental map.

00:17:43.220 --> 00:17:45.040
So, I'm just brainstorming,

00:17:45.040 --> 00:17:46.220
okay, as I'm looking through this.

00:17:46.220 --> 00:17:48.450
So, the first thing is I'm
looking at the marginalia.

00:17:48.450 --> 00:17:50.130
Is it the same on each page?

00:17:50.130 --> 00:17:51.920
- No, and almost on one of it

00:17:51.920 --> 00:17:52.753
we've actually identified

00:17:52.753 --> 00:17:53.887
what it's supposed to be.
- (gasps)

00:17:53.887 --> 00:17:55.570
This look like Bacon.

00:17:55.570 --> 00:17:56.620
I don't mean edible bacon.
- No, no, no...

00:17:56.620 --> 00:17:58.460
- I mean Francis Bacon.
- Francis Bacon.

00:17:58.460 --> 00:17:59.293
- Really?
- Right.

00:17:59.293 --> 00:18:01.060
- Yeah.
- Interesting.

00:18:01.060 --> 00:18:03.061
- Okay, Okay, keep going.
- Okay, I've never

00:18:03.061 --> 00:18:04.260
- I never heard that...

00:18:04.260 --> 00:18:08.330
- This could also be a Bacon Cipher.

00:18:08.330 --> 00:18:09.790
So, I tend to think about

00:18:09.790 --> 00:18:12.280
the psychology of whoever's creating this.

00:18:12.280 --> 00:18:13.390
Whoever's creating these

00:18:13.390 --> 00:18:15.557
is thinking, "Wow, I've
got a lot of smart people

00:18:15.557 --> 00:18:18.897
"that are gonna crack this
within weeks, you know, days.

00:18:18.897 --> 00:18:22.230
"I gotta make something really hard."

00:18:22.230 --> 00:18:24.920
Let's assume that it's the same person.

00:18:24.920 --> 00:18:26.430
That it's one person.

00:18:26.430 --> 00:18:28.240
I would wanna see all
the different puzzles

00:18:28.240 --> 00:18:30.590
that they've done, kind of all lined up.

00:18:30.590 --> 00:18:32.190
That's one of the ways I crack code.

00:18:32.190 --> 00:18:33.640
It's not cracking the code

00:18:33.640 --> 00:18:35.780
but understanding the mind
of who created the code,

00:18:35.780 --> 00:18:38.380
like some of these hacker
codes that I've cracked.

00:18:38.380 --> 00:18:40.440
Some of it is code
solving, but some of it is:

00:18:40.440 --> 00:18:41.540
Okay, who wrote this?

00:18:41.540 --> 00:18:42.980
Okay, it was this guy,

00:18:42.980 --> 00:18:44.490
who's the guy that runs the convention.

00:18:44.490 --> 00:18:45.750
Okay, when did he write this?

00:18:45.750 --> 00:18:47.840
He probably wrote it the
day before the convention

00:18:47.840 --> 00:18:49.980
'cause he was late on everything else.

00:18:49.980 --> 00:18:51.150
So, he's sleep deprived,

00:18:51.150 --> 00:18:52.400
he's probably drunk.
(Nox laughs)

00:18:52.400 --> 00:18:54.660
What kinds of mistakes
are they gonna make?

00:18:54.660 --> 00:18:56.916
Instead of shifting left,
they're gonna shift right.

00:18:56.916 --> 00:18:59.420
There's the word 'the', right?

00:18:59.420 --> 00:19:00.253
And then the rest of it

00:19:00.253 --> 00:19:01.937
just kind of zips open, right?
- Then it goes.

00:19:01.937 --> 00:19:03.593
- But what if there is no answer?

00:19:04.520 --> 00:19:07.380
What if someone created
this and they just,

00:19:07.380 --> 00:19:09.440
they're obviously computer savvy

00:19:09.440 --> 00:19:13.240
and maybe they wrote something
to generate random runes.

00:19:13.240 --> 00:19:18.240
So, this dump may not be solvable.

00:19:18.330 --> 00:19:21.270
- Yeah, it absolutely crossed my mind

00:19:21.270 --> 00:19:22.110
and kinda horrified me

00:19:22.110 --> 00:19:25.110
that I might be working
on something impossible.

00:19:25.110 --> 00:19:26.470
- You wanna see this solved.

00:19:26.470 --> 00:19:29.060
I can tell, you got the fire, why?

00:19:29.060 --> 00:19:32.320
- I mean, for me, in the long run,

00:19:32.320 --> 00:19:34.100
if say, Cicada's unsolvable,

00:19:34.100 --> 00:19:35.600
if I never find out who it is,

00:19:36.790 --> 00:19:38.510
the things I have learned,

00:19:38.510 --> 00:19:40.830
especially, and the people I have met

00:19:40.830 --> 00:19:42.590
and learned from them along the way

00:19:42.590 --> 00:19:43.950
has been so worth it

00:19:43.950 --> 00:19:46.220
that if it's the 17 year
old kid laughing about it,

00:19:46.220 --> 00:19:49.915
that's fine 'cause I learned
this much about cryptography.

00:19:49.915 --> 00:19:52.740
(slightly upbeat music)

00:19:52.740 --> 00:19:55.030
- Elonka made a great point
when she said we needed

00:19:55.030 --> 00:19:56.830
to get into the mind of the creator.

00:20:02.520 --> 00:20:04.480
We found a great potential lead.

00:20:04.480 --> 00:20:07.340
He's one of the world's top puzzle makers

00:20:07.340 --> 00:20:10.160
and he created a game with
a connection to Cicada

00:20:10.160 --> 00:20:11.083
we can't ignore.

00:20:17.000 --> 00:20:18.880
Adrian Hon is a leader in the field

00:20:18.880 --> 00:20:22.800
of what's called ARGs, or
alternate reality games.

00:20:22.800 --> 00:20:25.450
His clients include
corporations and media outlets

00:20:25.450 --> 00:20:28.100
looking to build buzz
around their content.

00:20:28.100 --> 00:20:29.330
And his games have been played

00:20:29.330 --> 00:20:32.000
by millions of devoted fans.

00:20:32.000 --> 00:20:33.660
Does it surprise you that

00:20:33.660 --> 00:20:37.250
puzzles and secret codes so appeals to us?

00:20:37.250 --> 00:20:39.800
- People want to believe
there is a sense of order

00:20:39.800 --> 00:20:42.660
in the universe and that
you have people who,

00:20:42.660 --> 00:20:44.010
from the dawn of time,

00:20:44.010 --> 00:20:44.843
they look up and they look at the stars

00:20:44.843 --> 00:20:46.717
and they think, "Why are
the stars in these shapes?"

00:20:46.717 --> 00:20:48.650
"Why is there a flood today?"

00:20:48.650 --> 00:20:50.860
There's always been this appeal of people

00:20:50.860 --> 00:20:52.170
trying to understand the world

00:20:52.170 --> 00:20:54.870
and other people trying to create puzzles

00:20:54.870 --> 00:20:58.040
to hide knowledge, maybe
to make themselves seem

00:20:58.040 --> 00:21:00.460
more mysterious or more
powerful than they really are.

00:21:00.460 --> 00:21:03.040
- In early 2011, your approach to create

00:21:03.040 --> 00:21:05.030
a suite of puzzles in support

00:21:05.030 --> 00:21:06.980
of this BBC show on mathematics

00:21:06.980 --> 00:21:09.532
called The Code.
- Correct

00:21:09.532 --> 00:21:10.365
(mystical music)

00:21:10.365 --> 00:21:12.280
- They want to make mathematics

00:21:12.280 --> 00:21:14.820
more interesting for a younger audience.

00:21:14.820 --> 00:21:16.130
Young people like games,

00:21:16.130 --> 00:21:18.880
so let's see if we can go and turn

00:21:18.880 --> 00:21:21.183
learning how mathematics
interacts with nature

00:21:21.183 --> 00:21:22.403
into the TV show.

00:21:23.580 --> 00:21:26.150
Everything from prime numbers,

00:21:26.150 --> 00:21:29.330
geometry, and how birds flock together

00:21:29.330 --> 00:21:31.797
and we think, "How can
we use those concepts

00:21:31.797 --> 00:21:35.917
"in a treasure hunt and
weave them into the program?"

00:21:36.760 --> 00:21:38.700
- As you know, the one
that interests us most

00:21:38.700 --> 00:21:39.843
is the very first one.

00:21:43.800 --> 00:21:44.810
Why cicadas?

00:21:44.810 --> 00:21:48.070
- Cicadas are known for
having a gestation period

00:21:48.070 --> 00:21:49.590
which is linked to prime numbers.

00:21:49.590 --> 00:21:51.457
Prime numbers are at the heart of nature

00:21:51.457 --> 00:21:53.530
and the heart of mathematics.

00:21:53.530 --> 00:21:56.550
- That puzzle comes out in June 2011.

00:21:56.550 --> 00:21:57.383
- Yeah.

00:21:57.383 --> 00:21:59.750
- Six months later, Cicada 3301

00:21:59.750 --> 00:22:01.633
makes its international debut.

00:22:02.670 --> 00:22:03.990
- It's a big coincidence.

00:22:03.990 --> 00:22:06.550
- There are some people who
have brought up the fact

00:22:06.550 --> 00:22:08.950
that whoever's behind Cicada 3301

00:22:08.950 --> 00:22:11.980
would have to be a very
accomplished game maker.

00:22:11.980 --> 00:22:13.300
- Sure.
- You would be a candidate

00:22:13.300 --> 00:22:14.670
to be that person.

00:22:14.670 --> 00:22:17.540
- That's true, I mean, Cicada 3301

00:22:17.540 --> 00:22:20.410
has a lot in common with
the games we've made.

00:22:20.410 --> 00:22:21.950
I think that's one big
difference (chuckles)

00:22:21.950 --> 00:22:24.720
is that normally when we
make alternate reality games,

00:22:24.720 --> 00:22:25.950
we do it for money.

00:22:25.950 --> 00:22:28.850
And it's not so clear to understand

00:22:28.850 --> 00:22:31.963
where the funding for
Cicada 3301 is coming from.

00:22:34.830 --> 00:22:38.860
- Would you be disappointed
or secretly thrilled,

00:22:38.860 --> 00:22:40.110
if 30 years from now,

00:22:40.110 --> 00:22:43.610
we still don't know who Cicada 3301 is?

00:22:43.610 --> 00:22:45.448
- I'd be delighted if no one knows

00:22:45.448 --> 00:22:46.490
who he is.
- Why?

00:22:46.490 --> 00:22:49.020
- Because I think that
when most of the puzzles

00:22:49.020 --> 00:22:50.420
that we make are made to be solved.

00:22:50.420 --> 00:22:52.560
And clearly, this is a very different one.

00:22:52.560 --> 00:22:54.490
Not everything has to be solved overnight,

00:22:54.490 --> 00:22:56.210
maybe it'll take 50
years or a hundred years

00:22:56.210 --> 00:22:58.183
and it's better for it.

00:22:59.165 --> 00:23:01.570
(soft calming music)

00:23:01.570 --> 00:23:03.110
- [Girl] My name is Shadowwalker

00:23:03.110 --> 00:23:05.000
and I live on the internet now.

00:23:05.000 --> 00:23:07.770
- [Man] The game is, "Can you
keep your identity secret?"

00:23:07.770 --> 00:23:10.050
- We do amazing things here at N.S.A.

00:23:10.050 --> 00:23:12.180
- Every time there's this
government intervention,

00:23:12.180 --> 00:23:14.028
you're going, okay which
side are we getting?

00:23:14.028 --> 00:23:15.710
- This is the world that we're living in.

00:23:15.710 --> 00:23:17.140
This is why we're going to need Cicada.

00:23:17.140 --> 00:23:18.760
- [Announcer] If you release this tool,

00:23:18.760 --> 00:23:20.260
and you save somebody's life,

00:23:20.260 --> 00:23:21.283
is it still a game?

00:23:26.100 --> 00:23:28.683
(upbeat music)

